# Real proof search with leanHaT and nanoCoP

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:swi_elixir, github: "davfuenmayor/swi_elixir", branch: "main"},
  {:kino, "== 0.19.0"}
])

project = Path.join(Mix.install_project_dir(), "deps/swi_elixir")

Kino.nothing()
```

## Introduction

Run two actual theorem provers on quantified formulas, inspect their search
activity, and compare two logics. The provers are [leanHaT](https://leancop.de/leanhat/)
and [nanoCoP-i-HT](https://www.leancop.de/ihat/).

Start with [Getting started](01_basic.livemd) if the wrapper is new to you.
Choose **Evaluate all** in Standalone. [All chapters and setup help](intro.livemd).

* [Preliminaries](#preliminaries-start-the-provers)
* [Follow a quantified function chain](#follow-a-quantified-function-chain)
* [Prove a finite pigeonhole principle](#prove-a-finite-pigeonhole-principle)
* [Compare two logics](#compare-two-logics)
* [Inspect a returned proof](#inspect-a-returned-proof)

## Preliminaries: start the provers

This cell loads both provers and starts a session. Leave `backend` at `:port`
for a separate SWI process, or choose `:nif` to run inside Livebook's BEAM.
The goals and calls are the same either way.

```elixir
import ExUnit.Assertions
backend = :port

vendor = fn path ->
  :json.encode(Path.join([project, "vendor", path])) |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
end
```

### How we ask the provers

These two adapters parse the formula in the prover's syntax and call its
search predicate. The engines themselves are vendored libraries. nanoCoP
uses an occurs check so unifying a variable with a term containing that
variable fails instead of creating a cycle.

```elixir
leanhat = ~s"""
:- module(leanhat, [prove_text/1]).
% The translator changes '=' precedence; restore it before reloading core.
:- op(700, xfx, =).
:- include(#{vendor.("leanHaT/leanhat_swi.pl")}).
:- include(#{vendor.("leanHaT/nanocop_tptp2.pl")}).

prove_text(Text) :-
    swi_bridge:read_goal(Text, leanhat, F, _), leancop_equal(F, Problem), prove(Problem).
"""
```

```elixir
nanocop = ~s"""
:- module(nanocop, [prove_text/2, prove_text/3]).
% The translator changes '=' precedence. Restore it before reconsulting core.
:- op(700, xfx, =).
% Keep the upstream load-time directive from changing the caller's flag.
:- current_prolog_flag(occurs_check, Flag), nb_setval(nanocop_load_flag, Flag).
:- include(#{vendor.("nanoCoP-i-HT/nanocopi20_swi.pl")}).
:- nb_getval(nanocop_load_flag, Flag), set_prolog_flag(occurs_check, Flag),
   nb_delete(nanocop_load_flag).
:- include(#{vendor.("nanoCoP-i-HT/nanocop_tptp2.pl")}).
% Upstream uses a dynamic scratch database. Each query engine owns its copy.
:- thread_local lit/4, pathlim/0.

prove_text(Text, Logic) :- prove_text(Text, Logic, _).

prove_text(Text, Logic, Proof) :-
    memberchk(Logic, [intu, ht]),
    setup_call_cleanup(
      (current_prolog_flag(occurs_check, Previous), set_prolog_flag(occurs_check, true)),
      (swi_bridge:read_goal(Text, nanocop, F, _),
       leancop_equal(F, Equal), leancop_intermed(Equal, Logic, Problem), prove(Problem, Proof)),
      set_prolog_flag(occurs_check, Previous)).
"""
```

The next program wraps each recursive search predicate with a counter.
Read the three parts of `setup_call_cleanup/3` as install, search, and
restore. The counter observes search without choosing its branches.

```elixir
diagnostics = ~S"""
:- module(prover_showcase, [run/7, source/3]).
:- use_module(library(prolog_wrap)).
:- use_module(leanhat).
:- use_module(nanocop).

core(leanhat, leanhat:prove(_,_,_,_), leanhat:prove/4).
core(nanocop, nanocop:prove(_,_,_,_,_,_,_,_,_,_), nanocop:prove/10).

source(Prover, File, Line) :-
    core(Prover, Head, _), clause(Head, _, Ref),
    clause_property(Ref, file(File)), clause_property(Ref, line_count(Line)), !.

% Run serially: wrappers modify predicates shared by all embedded engines.
% The counter itself belongs to the calling query engine.
run(Prover, Logic, Text, Result, Calls, Inferences, Proof) :-
    core(Prover, Head, PI),
    setup_call_cleanup(
      (nb_setval(showcase_calls, 0),
       wrap_predicate(Head, showcase, Wrapped,
         (prover_showcase:count, call(Wrapped)))),
      (statistics(inferences, Before),
       (once(search(Prover, Logic, Text, Proof)) -> Result = proved
       ; Result = exhausted, Proof = none),
       statistics(inferences, After), Inferences is After-Before,
       nb_getval(showcase_calls, Calls)),
      (unwrap_predicate(PI, showcase), nb_delete(showcase_calls))).

count :-
    (nb_current(showcase_calls, N) -> N1 is N+1, nb_setval(showcase_calls, N1) ; true).

search(leanhat, ht, Text, unavailable) :- leanhat:prove_text(Text).
search(nanocop, Logic, Text, Proof) :- nanocop:prove_text(Text, Logic, Proof).
"""
```

```elixir
dir = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "swi-livebook-provers-#{System.pid()}")
File.mkdir_p!(dir)

for {file, source} <- [
      {"leanhat.pl", leanhat},
      {"nanocop.pl", nanocop},
      {"prover_showcase.pl", diagnostics}
    ] do
  File.write!(Path.join(dir, file), source)
end

{:ok, session} = SWI.start_link(backend: backend)

try do
  :ok = SWI.load(session, Path.join(dir, "prover_showcase.pl"))
after
  File.rm_rf!(dir)
end
```

To switch, run the final close cell and rerun from here. With NIF, run one
prover experiment at a time because the diagnostics use shared predicates.

## Follow a quantified function chain

### What you will learn

Separate the formula you ask about from the prover that searches for its proof.
Our assumptions are `p(a)` and `∀x(p(x) → p(f(x)))`. Repeatedly applying the
second assumption should establish `p(f(f(f(f(a)))))`.

| Syntax              | Meaning                                  |
| ------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| `A,B`               | conjunction                              |
| `A;B`               | disjunction                              |
| `A=>B`              | implication                              |
| `~A`                | negation                                 |
| `all X:A`, `ex X:A` | universal and existential quantification |

Start at `p(a)`. Substituting `a` for `X` yields `p(f(a))`. What must you
substitute next? Generate the question in Elixir and let Prolog find the proof.

```elixir
chain = fn steps ->
  term = Enum.reduce(1..steps, "a", fn _, term -> "f(#{term})" end)
  "(p(a),(all X:(p(X)=>p(f(X)))))=>p(#{term})"
end

four_steps = chain.(4)
Kino.Text.new(four_steps)
```

Run the same formula through leanHaT in Here-and-There (HT) logic and through
nanoCoP in HT and intuitionistic logic. Every run checks its expected verdict
and records positive kernel call and inference counts.

```elixir
prove = fn prover, logic, formula ->
  text = :json.encode(formula) |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  goal = "prover_showcase:run(#{prover},#{logic},#{text},Result,Calls,Inferences,Proof)"
  [answer] = SWI.query(session, goal) |> Enum.to_list()
  assert answer["Calls"] > 0
  assert answer["Inferences"] > 0
  answer
end

run = fn name, prover, logic, formula, expected ->
  answer = prove.(prover, logic, formula)
  assert answer["Result"] == {:atom, expected}
  if prover == :nanocop and expected == "proved", do: assert(answer["Proof"] != [])

  %{
    problem: name,
    prover: prover,
    logic: logic,
    result: expected,
    kernel_calls: answer["Calls"],
    inferences: answer["Inferences"]
  }
end

chain_results =
  for {prover, logic} <- [leanhat: :ht, nanocop: :ht, nanocop: :intu] do
    run.("Function chain 4", prover, logic, four_steps, "proved")
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(chain_results)
```

The 20-step version gives the prover a longer chain. We run it in nanoCoP's
intuitionistic mode; HT search can be much more expensive on this family.

```elixir
long_chain = run.("Function chain 20", :nanocop, :intu, chain.(20), "proved")
Kino.DataTable.new([long_chain])
```

### Try it yourself

Change four steps to six. Predict whether the verdict or only the search work
should change. Update the displayed label when changing the formula.

<details>
<summary>Hint and expected result</summary>

The universal implication can be instantiated again at every step. The formula
still has a constructive proof. Calls and elapsed work can change substantially;
a timeout would mean the run is inconclusive.

</details>

## Prove a finite pigeonhole principle

Four pigeons each occupy one of three holes. Some pair must share a hole.
`o12` means pigeon 1 occupies hole 2. The antecedent states the alternatives
for every pigeon; the consequent lists all possible pair collisions.

For one hole, six pairs of pigeons could collide. Before reading the loop,
work out why three holes give eighteen candidate collisions.

```elixir
pigeonhole = fn holes ->
  placements =
    for pigeon <- 1..(holes + 1) do
      "(" <> Enum.map_join(1..holes, ";", &"o#{pigeon}#{&1}") <> ")"
    end

  collisions =
    for hole <- 1..holes,
        first <- 1..(holes + 1),
        second <- 1..(holes + 1),
        first < second,
        do: "(o#{first}#{hole},o#{second}#{hole})"

  "(" <> Enum.join(placements, ",") <> ")=>(" <> Enum.join(collisions, ";") <> ")"
end

pigeon_formula = pigeonhole.(3)
Kino.Text.new(pigeon_formula)
```

```elixir
pigeon_results =
  for logic <- [:ht, :intu] do
    run.("Four pigeons, three holes", :nanocop, logic, pigeon_formula, "proved")
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(pigeon_results)
```

## Compare two logics

HT and intuitionistic logic do not prove exactly the same formulas. Start
with prelinearity: could you construct a proof of either implication without
knowing anything about `p` or `q`? Compare that with excluded middle, which
fails in both logics here.

```elixir
logic_cases = [
  %{name: "Prelinearity", formula: "(p=>q);(q=>p)", ht: "proved", intu: "exhausted"},
  %{
    name: "Quantified witness",
    formula: "ex Y:all X:(p(Y)=>p(X))",
    ht: "proved",
    intu: "exhausted"
  },
  %{name: "Excluded middle", formula: "p; ~p", ht: "exhausted", intu: "exhausted"},
  %{
    name: "Double-negation elimination",
    formula: "(~ ~p)=>p",
    ht: "exhausted",
    intu: "exhausted"
  }
]

logic_results =
  for example <- logic_cases,
      {prover, logic} <- [leanhat: :ht, nanocop: :ht, nanocop: :intu] do
    run.(example.name, prover, logic, example.formula, Map.fetch!(example, logic))
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(logic_results)
```

`proved` means the invoked prover found a proof. `exhausted` means that its
search returned without one. The negative examples above have independent
countermodels; failure on an arbitrary new formula is not a general invalidity
test. nanoCoP's HT embedding is incomplete. A timeout raises `SWI.Error` and
leaves the logical verdict **unknown**; none is accepted as a passing result.

## Inspect a returned proof

nanoCoP can return its actual connection proof term through the wrapper.
Here we display an intuitionistic chain proof, so it concerns the formula
shown above directly. HT mode proves an intuitionistic translation instead.
The notebook displays the term without independently checking the certificate.

```elixir
proof = prove.(:nanocop, :intu, four_steps)
assert proof["Proof"] != []
Kino.Text.new(inspect(proof["Proof"], pretty: true, limit: :infinity))
```

leanHaT exposes success here; its search-bound argument is not a proof object.
The following table asks SWI where the real recursive kernels were loaded.

```elixir
kernels =
  for {prover, expected} <- [leanhat: "leanhat_swi.pl", nanocop: "nanocopi20_swi.pl"] do
    [row] =
      SWI.query(session, "prover_showcase:source(#{prover},File,Line)") |> Enum.to_list()

    {:atom, file} = row["File"]
    assert Path.basename(file) == expected
    %{prover: prover, loaded_kernel: Path.basename(file)}
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(kernels)
```

Kernel counts demonstrate execution. They include failed branches; inference
counts also include parsing, preprocessing, and diagnostics. They are not an
independent soundness check or a performance comparison without instrumentation.

